{
  "project": {
    "title": "Rubric-Aware Agentic Proposal Builder with Human-in-the-Loop Critique and Verifiable Revision",
    "keywords": "research proposal generation, agentic AI, rubric-aware evaluation, human-in-the-loop revision, citation grounding, verifiable LLM systems",
    "abstract": "This project proposes an agentic, rubric-aware system that helps researchers transform a rough research idea into a complete, citation-grounded research proposal. The system decomposes proposal writing into specialized stages: requirement extraction, section planning, literature and source grounding, draft generation, rubric-based critique, human feedback incorporation, and verifiable revision. Unlike generic large language model writing assistants, the proposed system explicitly represents proposal requirements as a checklist and uses iterative critique loops to detect missing sections, unsupported claims, weak novelty statements, and evaluation gaps. A human-in-the-loop interface allows users to accept, reject, or modify agent suggestions while preserving an auditable revision history. The expected outcome is a prototype that improves proposal completeness, alignment with rubrics, citation traceability, and user efficiency compared with baseline LLM drafting workflows.",
    "problem": "Researchers, students, and research teams often begin with incomplete or informal project ideas but must produce structured proposals that satisfy specific rubric requirements, include well-motivated research gaps, relate the idea to prior work, and provide credible methods, timelines, risks, and evaluation plans. General-purpose LLM tools can generate fluent drafts, but they often miss required sections, invent or misattribute citations, fail to connect novelty to prior work, and provide limited support for iterative, auditable revision. The target context is early-stage research proposal development in academic, educational, and applied R&D settings where users need both writing support and verifiable alignment with proposal criteria.",
    "novelty": "Prior work includes LLM-based writing assistants, retrieval-augmented generation systems, automated peer review or critique tools, and agentic frameworks such as ReAct-style reasoning, AutoGPT-like task decomposition, and multi-agent orchestration. Comparable tools can draft text, retrieve papers, or provide general feedback, but they usually do not combine rubric-aware requirement tracking, citation-grounded proposal generation, human-in-the-loop decision points, and a verifiable revision loop in one workflow. The novelty of this project is an integrated proposal-building agent that treats the rubric as an executable specification: it maps each proposal requirement to generated content, critiques each section against explicit criteria, links claims to source evidence, records user decisions, and reruns targeted revisions until the proposal reaches a measurable completeness and grounding threshold.",
    "goal": "The goal is to design, implement, and evaluate a prototype system that converts a rough research idea into a complete research proposal while improving rubric coverage, citation grounding, revision transparency, and user control compared with a single-pass LLM drafting baseline.",
    "method": "The system will use a modular agent workflow. First, an intake agent extracts the topic, target context, constraints, and checklist requirements from the user’s rough idea. Second, a planning agent creates a proposal outline and identifies missing information. Third, a retrieval agent gathers candidate sources from scholarly search APIs or a curated paper corpus and attaches evidence snippets to relevant claims. Fourth, a drafting agent generates proposal sections including abstract, motivation, novelty, method, timeline, evaluation, risks, resources, and references. Fifth, a rubric critic agent scores each section against explicit criteria such as completeness, specificity, citation support, feasibility, and internal consistency. Sixth, a human-in-the-loop decision layer presents suggestions, alternatives, and questions to the user. Seventh, a revision agent applies accepted feedback and produces a traceable revision log linking changes to rubric items, user decisions, and source evidence. The prototype can be implemented as a web application or notebook-based interface using an LLM API, retrieval-augmented generation, structured JSON state, citation metadata, and automated validation checks.",
    "figure": "The figure should show an end-to-end architecture diagram with seven connected modules: User Rough Idea and Rubric Input → Requirement Extraction Agent → Proposal Planning Agent → Retrieval and Citation Grounding Agent → Drafting Agent → Rubric Critique Agent → Human Decision Interface → Revision Agent → Final Proposal and Audit Log. Feedback arrows should connect the critique, human interface, and revision modules to indicate iterative improvement. The diagram should also show an evidence store feeding the drafting and critique stages. Caption: \"Figure 1. Rubric-aware agentic proposal workflow. The system converts a rough idea and proposal checklist into a structured, citation-grounded proposal through retrieval, drafting, automated critique, human decision-making, and verifiable revision.\"",
    "expectedResults": "Expected results include: (1) a working prototype that accepts a rough research idea and checklist and outputs a structured proposal; (2) a rubric-to-section mapping that shows which requirements are satisfied; (3) citation-grounded claims with source metadata or source notes; (4) an iterative critique and revision mechanism that improves proposal quality over multiple turns; and (5) an evaluation report comparing the system against baseline LLM drafting. Research milestones include requirements analysis, workflow design, prototype implementation, retrieval and citation grounding integration, rubric critic development, user interface development, evaluation, and final documentation.",
    "timeline": "Estimated 12-week timeline: Weeks 1–2: define proposal rubric, target users, evaluation criteria, and baseline workflows. Weeks 3–4: design system architecture, JSON project state, agent roles, and prompt templates. Weeks 5–6: implement intake, planning, drafting, and structured output generation. Weeks 7–8: integrate retrieval, citation metadata, and claim-source linking. Weeks 9–10: implement rubric critique, revision loop, and human decision interface. Week 11: run evaluation using sample rough ideas and human or expert ratings. Week 12: analyze results, document limitations, prepare final proposal, prototype demo, and release plan.",
    "evaluation": "The evaluation will compare the proposed system with a baseline single-pass LLM proposal generator and, if feasible, a generic chat-based iterative workflow. Test inputs will include multiple rough research ideas across technical and interdisciplinary domains. Metrics will include rubric coverage, section completeness, citation precision or source traceability, novelty clarity, evaluation-plan quality, number of unsupported claims, user editing effort, time to usable draft, and human-rated proposal quality. Automatic checks can measure required-section presence, citation coverage, and consistency between rubric items and generated sections. Human evaluators can rate clarity, feasibility, novelty, and overall usefulness using a standardized rubric.",
    "risks": "Key risks include hallucinated or weak citations, overreliance on LLM-generated claims, poor retrieval quality, excessive workflow complexity, user fatigue from too many questions, and difficulty evaluating proposal quality objectively. Mitigations include using retrieval-grounded generation, requiring source metadata for factual claims, flagging unsupported claims, limiting open-ended questions to high-value missing information, maintaining an auditable revision log, comparing against clear baselines, and using both automatic rubric checks and human ratings.",
    "resources": "Required resources include access to an LLM API or open-source language model, a retrieval source such as Semantic Scholar, Crossref, OpenAlex, arXiv, institutional databases, or a curated paper corpus, a vector database or document index, a web or notebook interface, structured JSON storage for project state, and evaluation participants or expert reviewers. The release plan should include a prototype demo, prompt and workflow templates, anonymized evaluation examples, documentation, and, where licensing allows, open-source code for the agent orchestration and rubric validation components.",
    "references": "Assumed reference areas include retrieval-augmented generation, tool-using and reasoning agents, human-in-the-loop AI, LLM evaluation, automated writing assistance, and scholarly recommendation systems. Candidate sources to verify and cite include: Lewis et al. (2020) on retrieval-augmented generation; Yao et al. (2023) on ReAct reasoning and acting; Shinn et al. (2023) on Reflexion and verbal reinforcement learning for agents; Wu et al. (2023) or related work on AutoGen multi-agent conversation frameworks; Gao et al. (2023/2024) surveys on retrieval-augmented generation; Kasneci et al. (2023) on ChatGPT in education; and recent work on LLM hallucination, citation grounding, and AI-assisted scientific writing. Exact bibliographic details should be verified during the retrieval phase."
  },
